================================================================================
WHAT THIS MEASURED
================================================================================

1. Correctness first, and per set. 80 checks, 0 failures: on each of the three sets,
   native, ffi and ffi-hosttc are byte-identical to the incumbent, the decode round
   trip returns the same bytes, and the incumbent parses its own output. On ascii the
   incumbent is checked against manifest.json before it is used as the oracle for the
   other two, so the oracle is anchored where anchoring is possible.

2. Wire size. latin1 is 1.687 to 1.748 times ascii and wide is 2.373 to 2.495 times.
   The rust slice published 1.70-1.75 and 2.39-2.50 from its own generator over the
   same description, and the two agree to three digits. That is a cheap but real R1
   check: two slices' value rules produce the same strings.

3. THE ANSWER TO SHAPES.MD'S SENTENCE, and it is different for the two directions.

   THE ENCODE RATIO IS ALMOST ENTIRELY A FACT ABOUT THE CONTENT SET.
   P1.2 ffi/pb goes 0.988 (ascii) -> 0.167 (latin1) -> 0.114 (wide). Every
   string-bearing payload does the same thing. The published C++ encode column is an
   ASCII column and nothing else.

   THE DECODE RATIO IS NOT. P1.2 ffi/pb goes 0.656 -> 0.671 -> 0.546, and no payload
   moves by more than about 0.15 across all three sets. The decode column survives
   being read without its content set; the encode column does not.

4. WHY, and this is the part that keeps the encode number honest. protobuf C++
   VALIDATES UTF-8 WHEN IT SERIALISES a `string` -- verified in the generated code,
   not inferred: shapes.pb.cc calls WireFormatLite::VerifyUtf8String(..., SERIALIZE)
   unconditionally on every string field, 37 call sites. ABI v1 says the core does
   not, because the encoder's check bought nothing and the parser cannot trust the
   wire anyway. So most of that column is a check the core SKIPS, and reporting
   `ffi` against `pb` alone on these sets would publish a policy difference as codec
   speed.

   The like-for-like row is `ffi-valtc`, the core's own validating transcoder:

     P1.2   ascii  valtc/pb 1.179   latin1 0.422   wide 0.365
     P2.2   ascii  valtc/pb 1.043   latin1 0.479   wide 0.421
     P3.1   ascii  valtc/pb 1.389   latin1 0.551   wide 0.505
     P4.1   ascii  valtc/pb 0.995   latin1 0.626   wide 0.530

   So even doing the same work, the core is at parity or slightly worse on ascii and
   roughly TWICE AS FAST on latin1 and wide. That is a statement about the two
   validators and it agrees with logs/cpp/utf8.log measuring them directly: protobuf's
   IsStructurallyValidUTF8 is 2.16 to 2.24 ns/byte on those sets where this slice's
   lead-byte table is 1.19 to 1.74.

   Growth against each arm's OWN ascii row, which separates the three effects:

     arm         P1.2 latin1   P1.2 wide    what grows
     ffi              1.038       1.054     width only -- the core does not validate
     ffi-valtc        2.197       2.819     width + the core's validator
     pb               6.134       9.116     width + protobuf's validator + its own
                                            per-string costs

5. P6.1 is the control and it behaves like one. M6 is packed scalars with one string
   per batch: its wire size moves 1.058 and 1.117 where the others move 1.7 and 2.4,
   and its ffi encode moves 0.95 to 0.99. A table where every payload moved by the
   same factor would be measuring the harness.

WHAT IS NOT MEASURED

  * The floor. These are data sets, not language levels, and the C++11 arm compiles
    the same traversal; nothing here would distinguish them, so there is no c11 build
    of this arm.
  * P5.x. M5 is bulk `bytes` and a content set says what is in the STRINGS, so there
    is nothing for it to change. Deliberately absent rather than silently missing.
  * A no-validation protobuf. protobuf C++ offers no supported way to turn its
    serialize-side check off, and R14 says the baseline is what production runs, so
    the `pb` column keeps it and `ffi-valtc` is the row that isolates it.
  * Anything about a narrowing transcoder, which is the thing these sets price in a
    managed host and cannot price here.

